#!/bin/bash
# 713 (Sun 22 Nov 2026 07:42, Peter: "Yes" to the map; "data should be telling us"). No rules brought to the data: measure, on
# everything we hold, how often things happened against how often the price said they would. Paper only. No orders. Nothing deleted.
mkdir -p /root/pe-sports/map /root/pe-builder/runs
cat > /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_map.md <<'BRIEF'
You are a Builder on Peter Edwards' trading server, session 19. Work in /root/pe-sports/map (new files, schema 'map'). Read /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_league.md first (HARD RULES, STANDING RULE 708: paper only, never an order endpoint, nothing deleted, exchanges only). Memory: the server has 18 GB and other things run on it. Do the heavy lifting inside the database in chunks (by sport and month), with statement timeouts, never pull a whole table into Python, one heavy query at a time, and keep the recorders and the paper trader alive (check free memory before each big step).
PETER'S ORDER: stop shoehorning strategies into the data. The data should tell us. So bring NO rules. Take one measurement everywhere: how often did it happen, against how often the price said it would.
THE DATA: (1) the history files - bf_hist_other (about 282 million rows of last traded prices, every sport, with bf_hist_result) and the football history tables (bf_hist_price or whatever holds soccer; the eight seasons of results with Betfair pre-match prices; football-data files with closing prices), Betfair starting-price files for racing (racing_bsp and the daily BSP files), greyhounds; years deep. Last traded prices are NOT money and are never reported as £ - here they are used only as 'what the market believed at that moment', which is what they are good for; (2) our own recorded real prices since 31 Oct (stream_ticks, book_snap, ip_tape, xx_tape and the archive days), for the final check at prices that could really be taken.
THE MEASUREMENT (table map.calib): for every settled selection, at each fixed moment that exists for it (24 h, 6 h, 1 h, 10 min, 2 min before the start; the start itself / starting price; and in play at fixed clock minutes for sports with a clock, or at each tenth of elapsed market time otherwise - defined ONLY by the clock, never by when the market ended), take the price the market believed, turn all runners' prices into chances that sum to 100% (remove the over-round properly; say how), and record chance believed, whether it won, and the cell it belongs to. Cells: sport x market type x moment x chance band (5-point bands; 1-point bands under 10% and over 90%) - and then each of these, one at a time, as a further split: money matched (five bands), number of runners, country / league / course, day of week, hour, month, favourite rank, how the price moved since the previous moment (shortened, steady, drifted - three bands each way), and for racing: race type, distance band, going, field size; for football: league level, home or away or draw, goal line; tennis: round, best of 3 or 5 where known; others as available. Then the facts we hold joined in the same way where they exist for enough markets (form, league position, goals for and against, expected goals; draw, trainer, jockey; score state in play) - one at a time, never pairs or threes in this session.
FOR EVERY CELL: markets, believed chance, actual rate, the gap in points and as £ per £100 for a back at that belief and for a lay, a confidence interval that respects one market = one observation (runners in a market are not independent; cluster by market, and by day for the interval), and the gap AFTER 2% commission.
HONESTY THAT MAKES IT USABLE: (a) split time in three, fixed now: oldest third = where gaps are first seen; middle third = do they persist; newest third = do they still. A gap counts only if it has the same sign in all three and its interval excludes zero on the newest. (b) Count how many cells were looked at and say how many would show a gap this size by chance; rank survivors by how far they clear that. (c) Then, for the survivors only, the check at real prices: on our recorded days since 31 Oct, back (or lay, under 5.0 only) at the price actually on offer at that moment with the money shown, 2% commission, and report £ per £100 and £ a day at a £40 stake, with the gap between back and lay shown separately, so we can see which gaps are bigger than the cost of taking them. Backs first. A gap that exists in belief but is smaller than the cost of trading is listed as 'real but not tradeable by taking', with the size of the shortfall.
WHAT THE DATA SAYS, in plain English: /root/pe-queue/reports/the_map.md, two screens at most. Line 1: how many markets and how many years the map covers, how many cells were measured, how many gaps survive all three periods beyond chance. Then the survivors as sentences a non-trader can read, biggest money first, each: 'In <sport, market, moment>, things the market rated at about X% happened Y% of the time over N markets (old/middle/new: a/b/c); backing at the price on offer since 31 Oct kept £z per £100 on m markets' - and whether it is a back or a lay, a bet held to the result, and how many chances a day. Then the biggest surprises in the other direction: where the market is almost exactly right (so we stop looking there). Then what could not be measured and what is missing for it. Do NOT propose strategies, names, or plans; describe what is there. Peter's two known facts to check by name without favour: favourites in small football markets; long shots in racing.
ALSO: for each survivor that is a back (or a lay under 5.0) and positive at real prices, append it, frozen exactly as measured, to the trial list (see /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_trial.md and grid.trial_trades / reports/trial_today.md) so today's matches start scoring it; say which were added.
ORDER OF WORK, pushing the file as it grows: football and British/Irish racing first (first version of the_map.md within 4 hours), then tennis, cricket, greyhounds, golf and the rest, then the facts layered on. Commit and push after each sport: cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/the_map.md && git commit -qm "the map" && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q. When all sports and the facts are done, write the final version and end the session.
BRIEF
cat > /root/pe-builder/run_map.sh <<'SH'
#!/bin/bash
CL=$(cat /root/pe-watchman/claude_path); ts=$(date -u +%F_%H%M)
cd /root/pe-sports/map && timeout -k 60 36000 "$CL" -p "$(cat /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_map.md)" --allowedTools "Bash,Read,Write,Edit,Glob,Grep" --max-turns 800 --output-format text > /root/pe-builder/runs/${ts}_map.log 2>&1
echo "map session ended $(date -u '+%F %H:%M') exit $?" >> /root/pe-builder/sessions.log
SH
chmod +x /root/pe-builder/run_map.sh
nohup setsid /root/pe-builder/run_map.sh > /dev/null 2>&1 < /dev/null & sleep 15
echo "map session: $(pgrep -fc '[r]un_map.sh') | trial session: $(pgrep -fc '[r]un_builder_trial.sh') | free memory: $(free -g | awk '/Mem/{print $7" GB of "$2}') | $(date -u +%H:%M) UTC" > /root/pe-queue/reports/running_now.md
cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/running_now.md && git commit -qm "running now" -q && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q
